<BaseLayout title="About O2AT" description="The purpose, scope, author and current maturity of the Open Optical Audio Transport engineering proposal.">
  <div class="wrap page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>An open proposal for an optical link.</h1><p class="lead">Open Optical Audio Transport is a proposed Layer 2 audio interconnect centred on standard 1G SFP hardware and receiver-owned conversion timing.</p></div>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Purpose</p><h2>Define the transport before claiming the outcome.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The project explores whether a duplex optical Ethernet link can provide a more open and capable successor path for consumer optical audio, while preserving the signal isolation available at a fibre boundary.</p><p>The specification concentrates on packet format, capability negotiation, receiver credits, buffering, clock relationships and explicit endpoint limits. It does not claim that an open wire protocol alone improves sound, removes all interface jitter or solves every legacy format.</p><p>Project initiator and author: Tunji Durodola. The current specification is an engineering draft for review.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Scope and roadmap</p><h2>Measured implementation is the next evidence step.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The proposal starts with a point-to-point 1G optical profile, with optional 10G for aggregation and higher channel counts. A later ADAT/S/MUX bridge may be useful but is distinct from this framing and is not specified as a complete profile here.</p><p>Future work includes independent packet encoders and decoders, a physical reference design, clock and FIFO measurements, reference-loss qualification and cross-vendor interoperability. Each result must name the source revision and test conditions.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Project materials</p><h2>Read the source and join the review.</h2></div><ul class="link-list"><li><a href="/specification/"><span>Specification and source downloads</span><span>↗</span></a></li><li><a href="/build-and-test/"><span>Prototype validation plan</span><span>↗</span></a></li><li><a href="/contribute/"><span>Review and contribute</span><span>↗</span></a></li></ul></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
